{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 50, :comments ["<b>And the priest shall look, etc., and he shall shut it up.</b> One needs to know what reason the verse changed the ruling of the plague of a garment from the ruling of the plague of a person: that the plague of a person can exist without isolation when it is a confirmed plague, whereas the plague of a garment — even though it is a plague, as the verse states, ״נֶגַע צָרַעַת הִיא״ — even so, He did not command the priest to declare it impure but rather to shut it up. And it emerges according to this that the verse regards a person's garments more than the person himself. And it appears that since garments, when they are definitively declared [impure], that is their destruction — if on the first occasion they were definitively declared [impure], then there would be no remedy for a penitent to repent for the sin on account of which this plague came. And we have already established that HaShem does not desire the loss and destruction of a person's property. Go and learn from what they of blessed memory expounded (Torat Kohanim; Negaim 12:5) on the verse ״וְצִוָּה הַכֹּהֵן וּפִנּוּ אֶת הַבַּיִת״ — that the Holy One, blessed be He, has compassion even upon the insignificant property of the wicked. Therefore there is no plague in garments that is definitively declared [impure] in a single [inspection], unlike a person, for whom there is repentance and repair even after his definitive declaration [of impure] — for he is not destroyed in his definitive declaration but only separated, and upon his return the plague is removed from him and he becomes pure. For this reason there exists for [garment plague] a definitive declaration on the first occasion, and the ways of HaShem are upright." "<b>Thus concludes the section of Tazria.</b>"], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:56:21.294936893Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}